{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-three-parameters-wedderburn","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Proposition","doco:Section"],"label":"prop:three-parameters-wedderburn","kind":"proposition","title":"Three calibration parameters from Wedderburn-Artin decomposition of $H_3(q)$","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-calibration-inputs"],"tags":["wedderburn","calibration","irrep","hecke-algebra","young-symmetrizer","on-shell"],"leanRef":"qou:QOU.BraidKnot.three_parameters_wedderburn","text":"three-parameters-wedderburn.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"prop:three-parameters-wedderburn\"></a><p>The number of calibration inputs\n(<a href=\"#rem:calibration-inputs\">calibration inputs</a>)\nequals the number of <strong>irreducible representations</strong> of\n<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">H_3(R, q)</code>, which is the number of partitions of <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">3</code>.</p>\n<h2>Wedderburn-Artin decomposition</h2>\n<p>The Iwahori--Hecke algebra <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">H_3(R, q)</code> is semisimple for generic\n<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code> and decomposes via the Wedderburn theorem as:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">  H_3(R, q)\n  \\;\\cong\\;\n  \\underbrace{M_1(k)}_{\\lambda = (3)}\n  \\;\\oplus\\;\n  \\underbrace{M_2(k)}_{\\lambda = (2,1)}\n  \\;\\oplus\\;\n  \\underbrace{M_1(k)}_{\\lambda = (1{,}1{,}1)}</code></pre>\n<p>with dimensions <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1^2 + 2^2 + 1^2 = 1 + 4 + 1 = 6 = \\dim H_3(R, q)</code>.</p>\n<p>Each simple component corresponds to an irreducible\nrepresentation labelled by a partition <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\lambda \\vdash 3</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\">Partition</th>\n<th align=\"center\">Irrep</th>\n<th align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\dim V_\\lambda</code></th>\n<th align=\"center\">Eigenvalue of <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\sigma_i</code></th>\n<th align=\"left\">Physical sector</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(3)</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\">Symmetric</td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">+q</code></td>\n<td align=\"left\">Shape (deformation)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(2,1)</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\">Standard</td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">+q</code> and <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">-q^{-1}</code> mixed</td>\n<td align=\"left\">Scale (mass ruler)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(1{,}1{,}1)</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\">Alternating</td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">-q^{-1}</code></td>\n<td align=\"left\">Interaction (hadronic)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>On-shell projection</h2>\n<p>The Lagrangian endomorphism <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\Phi \\in \\mathrm{End}_{\\mathbf{C}}(A)</code>\n(<a href=\"#def:categorical-action\">categorical action</a>) decomposes\nover the three irreps via the central idempotents <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">e_\\lambda</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">  \\Phi\n  =\n  \\alpha_1 \\cdot e_{(3)}\n  +\n  \\alpha_2 \\cdot e_{(2,1)}\n  +\n  \\alpha_3 \\cdot e_{(1{,}1{,}1)}</code></pre>\n<p>The on-shell condition <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\star_q F = F</code>, observability\n<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{Hom}(\\mathbf{1}, \\mathrm{Ad}(A)) = 0</code>, and gauge\ninvariance reduce each component to one real scalar:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\">Component</th>\n<th align=\"center\">Raw dim</th>\n<th align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">-</code> Ad</th>\n<th align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">-</code> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\star</code></th>\n<th align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">-</code> gauge</th>\n<th align=\"center\">Surviving</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">M_1</code> (symmetric)</td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><strong><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">M_2</code> (standard)</td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">4</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><strong><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">M_1</code> (alternating)</td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><strong><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"><strong>Total</strong></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">6</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><strong><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">3</code></strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Three irreps, three surviving parameters, three calibration\ninputs.</p>\n<p>The physical identification of the three parameters, the sufficiency\nargument for all <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">A</code>, and the leptonic-sector cross-check are collected in\n<a href=\"#rem:three-parameters-wedderburn-context\">the three-parameter context</a>.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/three-parameters-wedderburn.md","heading":"Proposition","number":"13.2.6"}
